#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e8ad55-058a-5d71-ac05-3f8533f8bd46" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Small Positive Integer Power"}] :question/id #uuid "2777aa24-9055-55d3-9548-3aef0586bea7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3942" :question/problem "What is the value of $4\\textrm{i}^3$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$, where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{0} &= 1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i} = (-1) \\cdot \\textrm{i} =-\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} = (-1) \\cdot (-1) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\textrm{i}^{3} &= 4 \\cdot (-\\textrm{i}) =-4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "760a1a5b-4c06-547d-9248-a6acb97c4b84" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Positive Integer Power Using the Cyclic Property"}] :question/id #uuid "71af77a8-8b16-59f5-b6fb-0c344c3b9f90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3943" :question/problem "Simplify $\\textrm{i}^{15}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15$. Dividing the power by $4$, we get\n\n$$\n15\\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c51033-ad39-5776-9829-cf573214ef03" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Powers of i Using the Laws of Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "3bc3d3e9-6745-5021-b571-a301851d7bb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3944" :question/problem "If $a = \\textrm{i}^9$ and $b = \\textrm{i}^{7}$, then $ab =$" :question/worked-solution "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16$. Dividing the power by $4$, we get\n\n$$\n16\\div 4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Negative Power"}] :question/id #uuid "a1b59f3b-4378-5b1e-b21e-a865369e6e01" :question/math-academy-id "e-3964" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\textrm{i}^{-2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{i}^2}\n$$\n\nWe now recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{i}^2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-1)} = -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e8ad55-058a-5d71-ac05-3f8533f8bd46" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Small Positive Integer Power"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecda4efc-f543-5a75-8638-7f985bba9d1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-533" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\text{i}}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$"}]}
